    "dateIdentified": "2026-02-11",
    "contactPerson": "Stuart James",
    "roomCode": "CC.1N.403\nCC.1N.404",
    "roomName": "WAAPA Control Room Large\nWAAPA Control Room Small",
    "subject": "Less than ideal talkback mic position",
    "detail": "Currently there is an overhead room mic for talkback and lecture capture. Due to the large distance of this microphone from the lecturer or operator in the control room will require it to be gained high enough to cause feedback over both speakers and headphones right across the studio (as it will be picking up signal from the live room microphones through the studio monitor speakers). At a minimum, the position will also cause comb filtering (phase artefacts) when mixing the room microphone with any DAW audio. Furthermore, lecture capture may be compromised if the microphone is picking up students chattering as much as the lecturer's own voice.\nHaving a dedicated talkback microphone is essential. One potential solution is to have the ceiling mic going into the video capture with additional line in from the DAW (set as a preset routing), plus a regular dedicated TB mic at the mix position, that only goes to the headphones/studio floor.  WHS Issue",
    "priority": "2 - Urgent - Significant Impact",
